\chapter{The compiler SDK and its capability matrix}
\label{ch:111}
\index{The compiler SDK and its capability matrix}

SDK 2 exposes a C11 boundary to compiler services and keeps the ABI-v1 extension profile distinct. Installed CMake consumers select \OnxCodeTerm{On1xSDK 2} and link \OnxCodeTerm{On1x::SDK}. The project service and semantics/IR support have their own targets. C clients must link the C++ implementation and compatible runtime through a suitable toolchain.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
on1xc sdk info
on1xc sdk validate catalogue on1x/sdk/CAPABILITY_MATRIX.json
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Use the checked capability records to determine required input phase, schema and artifact kind. A tool can examine the catalogue without loading providers. In a versioned C consumer, initialize supported prefixes of the ABI records and use the documented lifetime rules for returned data. Check ordinary rejection paths before extending a host to a new phase. Inspect whether the operation supplies a borrowed compiler view or owns a result that needs release. Runtime embedding uses the independently installed ABI described in \cref{ch:114}.

\section{Review an SDK client's first request}
Suppose a client wants the immediate dominator of a block. In the
matrix, look under the analysis facility rather than assuming any
object called a block is a suitable input. The supported record names
verified MIR and a bounded analysis keyed by revision. A parsed source
tree with an identically numbered block would not meet that contract.
Nor does the ability to ask a dominator question supply a points-to
answer: the latter appears among the blockers. This is how to read a
partial facility row without either discarding its working subset or
granting its missing operations.

At the C boundary, imagine a newer host hands an older plugin
a function table whose last optional field did not exist when the
plugin was built. The plugin can consume its known prefix. In the
reverse situation a newer plugin must check that the entire desired
field is inside the older table's declared size before reading the
function pointer, then check that the pointer is nonnull. Checking only
the major version misses the shorter table; checking only the pointer
requires reading a field which might not exist. The v1 compatibility
route uses its own descriptor rather than casting it to a v2 layout.

For a practical check, ask what failure should be recorded if a valid
analysis runs out of its deterministic work allowance. The status is
LIMIT. It is evidence about resources available for that query, not a
claim that the program is malformed or the block has no dominator.

A cached answer still has to fit the caller's current limits. A small
second request cannot evade its budget just because a larger earlier
request paid to compute the facts.

